<h2 id="issues-tasks">Issues and Tasks</h2>
<p>
  The main components of the app are <strong>Issues</strong> and <strong>Tasks</strong>.
  Issues are similar to GitHub issues. Anyone with access can use them to report bugs or
  suggest improvements. On the other hand, tasks can only be created by
  <strong>Reviewers</strong> or <strong>Admins</strong>. Often times, they are created
  from an Issue. They lay out specific instructions on how to fix the Issues' bugs or
  complete their new features. Tasks can also be added independently.
</p>

<h3>Type Configuration</h3>

<p>
  Issues and Tasks are grouped by <strong>types</strong> (eg. Bug, Feature Request,
  Question). <strong>Admins</strong> have several color and icon options available in
  order to customize them:
</p>

<hr>

<h2 id="features-user-levels">User Levels</h2>
<p>
  There are four levels available. Here is a short description of each:
</p>

<h3>Admins</h3>
<p>
  One or two people responsible for the initial app setup and user
  management. They have the same abilities as
  <strong>Reviewers</strong>, but they can also
  edit/remove other people's issues, tasks, comments, etc.
</p>

<h3>Reviewers</h3>
<p>
  Basically, the project managers. They can do some app setup like create
  Categories and Projects. However, their main responsibility is managing
  issues and tasks.
</p>

<h3>Workers</h3>
<p>
  The busy bees working on tasks. In addition, they can report issues and
  self-assign tasks.
</p>

<h3>Reporters</h3>
<p>
  Everyone else can open issues, add comments, and see activity.
</p>

